.TH CSORT_GET_DEFAULT_COMPARISON_PROC 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
csort_get_default_comparison_proc \- get default comparison for a type
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/csort.h>

ccol_comparison_proc_t csort_get_default_comparison_proc(dummy_var);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro gives the default comparison function for a given type through
C11 _Generic, so the type of the dummy variable decides which comparison
function the macro selects.

The macro supports all the standard C integral types (char, signed char,
short, int, long, long long and the unsigned variants), the
floating-point types float, double and long double, and the string types
char* and const char*, with any signed or unsigned character type. A
pointer that is itself const or volatile, such as a
static const char *const, is a string type too. The comparator for a
string type puts a stored NULL before every string that is not NULL, and
that NULL is equal only to another NULL.

The macro supports an enumerated type too: C makes every enumerated type
compatible with one of the standard integer types, and the macro gives
the comparison function of that compatible type.

A NULL result for an unsupported type is part of the contract of this
macro, because a caller with its own fallback depends on that result; for
example, cvec_find compares such an element byte-wise. A caller with no
fallback must refuse the type at compile time rather than at run time, as
cvec_sort does.

A fixed-size char array (char[N]) is not a supported type, and it is a
different thing from a char* pointer variable. The macro gives NULL for
such an array, as it does for every other unsupported type. The inline
byte content of such an array is not a char* value, and
_csort_default_string_comparison_proc dereferences a pointer, so its
contract does not hold for an array. Sort a collection of fixed-size
string buffers with an explicit comparator that you write yourself, and
give that comparator directly to cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc() or
to csort_sort().
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dummy_var  A variable of the type that you want to compare. The macro does
           not evaluate it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Gives a pointer to the correct comparison function
Gives NULL when the macro does not support the type
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro uses the variable only to find the type and does not evaluate
it, so dummy_var can be any expression of the type that you want to
compare: an rvalue such as a cast or a literal is legal, and the
expression does not need an address.
For a real char* or const char* pointer variable, the macro gives
_csort_default_string_comparison_proc. That includes a pointer variable
that is itself const or volatile
For a fixed-size char array (char[N]), the macro gives NULL, not
_csort_default_string_comparison_proc.
For an enumerated type, the macro gives the comparison function of the
standard integer type that is compatible with that enumerated type
For an integral type, the comparison is three-way: (a > b) - (a < b)
For a floating-point type (float, double or long double), a NaN operand is
greater than every value that is not NaN and equal only to another NaN,
so the result is always a real total order.
Every default comparator reads its two operands byte-wise, so an operand
address needs neither the alignment nor the effective type of the
element, which matters for a custom getter over a packed buffer.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
// Get the comparator for a string
char* dummy_str;
ccol_comparison_proc_t cmp = csort_get_default_comparison_proc(dummy_str);
// cmp now points to _csort_default_string_comparison_proc

// Get the comparator for a double
double dummy_double;
cmp = csort_get_default_comparison_proc(dummy_double);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR csort_sort (3),
.BR cvec_sort (3)
